 "fieldNotes": "From the track: start 5:36 PM at Spooner Summit, 7,127 ft; high point 7,280 ft about a mile in; long descent to 4,814 ft at James Lee Park. 18.2 miles, 2 h 34 m total, 1 h 52 m moving, 518 ft of climbing, top speed 18.8 mph. Finished 8:11 PM.\n\nFrom Christian (Oct 1): Clear Creek Trail from Spooner to James Lee. His wife drove them up and dropped them off. Riding with his brother and nephew, visiting from Buffalo, NY for the first time; they hadn't ridden bikes in years and did a great job, the most fun they'd had in a long time. Christian had done it a few times and loved doing it with them for their first. The 6:11 stop was both a breather and the view. They needed lights for the last part: he was worried they wouldn't get out of the alpine section into the easier desert riding below before dark. Lights on for the last stretch down to the James Lee part of the trail and over to James Lee Park. Brought home: a few bumps, sore muscles, and a great story.",
 "post": {
  "title": "Clear Creek after dark",
  "body": "My brother and my nephew came out from Buffalo, New York, for their first visit, and I wanted them to ride Clear Creek. I've ridden it a few times: you start high at Spooner Summit and spend most of the evening going down, from the pines into the desert. Neither of them had been on a bike in years.\n\nMy wife drove the three of us up and dropped us at the trailhead at 5:36. The first mile is the only real climb, about 150 feet up to 7,280 feet, and then the trail turns downhill and mostly stays that way.\n\nWe stopped under the big pines at 6:11, half for a breather and half for the view. By 6:22 we were up on a pile of granite with the forest falling away behind us, grinning at the camera.\n\nThe part I worried about was the light. The top of Clear Creek is the alpine section, and it's the part you want to be out of before dark; below it the trail eases into desert riding. The lights came on for the last stretch down to the James Lee end of the trail and over to James Lee Park. We rolled in at 8:11.\n\nBy the numbers: 18.2 miles, about 2,300 feet lower than where we started, two and a half hours start to finish.\n\nThey came home with a few bumps, sore muscles and a great story, and they said it was the most fun they'd had in a long time. I've ridden this trail a few times, and I loved doing it with them for their first.\n\nWhat I learned: start an evening ride earlier than you think, and bring lights anyway. And a trail you know is a different trail when you're showing it to someone."
 },
 "episode": {
  "title": "Clear Creek after dark",
  "audio": null,
  "script": "It's five thirty-six on a Saturday evening in September, at the top of Spooner Summit. Christian's wife has just driven three men and three bikes up the mountain, and she's leaning out of the truck window to see them off.\n\nThe three men are Christian, his brother, and his nephew. The brother and the nephew are from Buffalo, New York. This is their first time out here. It's also the first time either of them has been on a bike in years.\n\n***\n\nThe first mile goes up. Not far, a hundred and fifty feet or so, to seven thousand two hundred and eighty. And then the Clear Creek Trail turns downhill and keeps that promise for most of the next eighteen miles.\n\nAt eleven minutes past six they stop under the big pines. Half breather, half view. Eleven minutes after that they're up on a pile of granite, forest falling away behind them, three grins in one photo.\n\n***\n\nHere's what Christian is quietly worried about. He's done this ride a few times. He knows the top is the alpine section, and that's the part you want behind you before dark. Below it, the trail eases out into the desert. And the sun is going.\n\nThe sun goes down anyway. The lights come on for the last stretch, down to the James Lee end of the trail and across to James Lee Park, where they roll in at eleven minutes past eight.\n\n***\n\nEighteen miles. Two and a half hours. About two thousand three hundred feet lower than where they started. A few bumps, some very sore muscles, and, according to the two visitors from Buffalo, the most fun they've had in a long time.\n\nChristian has ridden Clear Creek before. He'd never ridden it with them. That was the part he loved."
 },
